This post exists to show every building block a writing post supports. Scroll through it, hover the tick marks on the left to jump between sections, click any image to open it full size, and try the width and focus controls next to the date.

# Part one: text

Everything in this part is plain Markdown, rendered with the site's typography.

## Inline formatting

Text can be **bold**, *italic*, ***both***, or ~~struck through~~. Short snippets of code such as `pnpm dev` or `getCollection("writing")` sit inline without breaking the line. Links look like [the Astro docs](https://docs.astro.build) and always keep their underline so they stay obvious.

### Combining styles

Styles nest: **bold text with `inline code` inside**, *italic text with a [link](https://astro.build) inside*, and ~~struck text~~ next to **bold** again.

## Lists

An unordered list for loose points:

- Content lives in Markdoc files, edited through Keystatic
- Pages are static, so there is nothing to wait for
  - Images are optimised at build time
  - Fonts are self-hosted
- Interactivity is a few lines of Alpine.js

An ordered list for steps:

1. Write the post in Keystatic
2. Add tags so it shows up in the filters
3. Commit, and let the deploy do the rest
   1. Preview the branch first
   2. Merge when it looks right

## Quotes and rules

> A blockquote stays on the text column, with a tint and a rule on its left edge, so it lines up with the paragraphs around it.

A horizontal rule separates two thoughts without a new heading.

---

Like that.

# Part two: media

Images, columns and carousels all keep their aspect ratio.

## A single image

![A placeholder illustration](../../assets/writing/example-wide.svg "Click the image to open it full size. This caption stays on the text column.")

Images fill the text column. Pressing Enter or Space on a focused image opens the same dialog; Escape closes it and puts focus back where it was.

## Two columns

{% columns %}
{% column %}
The left column holds a short block of text. It sits beside the right column on wide screens and stacks above it on small ones, which makes it handy for an image with a few words of context.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
![Right column image](../../assets/writing/example-slide-1.svg "Images in columns open the lightbox too.")
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

## A carousel

{% carousel caption="Three placeholder slides, each cropped to its own aspect ratio." %}
{% slide ratio="original" %}
![First slide](../../assets/writing/example-slide-1.svg)
{% /slide %}

{% slide ratio="1/1" %}
![Second slide](../../assets/writing/example-slide-2.svg)
{% /slide %}

{% slide ratio="3/4" %}
![Third slide](../../assets/writing/example-slide-3.svg)
{% /slide %}
{% /carousel %}

The track runs the full width of the page, over the sidebar where they meet, while the first slide lines up with the text. The arrows above it move one slide at a time, and the track itself can be scrolled with the keyboard once it has focus.

# Part three: code and data

## Code blocks

Code blocks have line highlights, inserted and deleted lines, a copy button and a wrap toggle.

```ts {% mark="2" ins="3" del="4" %}
export function greet(name: string) {
  return `Hello, ${name}. This line is long enough to need horizontal scrolling inside the code block on a narrow screen.`;
  const added = true;
  const removed = false;
}
```

A diff block colours additions and removals without any extra markup, and `wrap=true` starts it with long lines wrapped:

```diff {% wrap=true %}
- const old = 1;
+ const next = 2;
 const same = 3;
```

Shell commands get a terminal frame:

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm dev
```

